CAT = "sgresLandLaw"; SEG = "LND"
Q = [
("Which of these is real property rather than personal property?",
 "A strata office unit", ["A company car", "Units in a REIT", "A sofa in the unit"],
 "Real property is land and things attached to it, including a strata unit. Cars, furniture and REIT units are personal property, even though a REIT owns real estate."),
("A right that can bind a later buyer of the land is a:",
 "Proprietary right", ["Personal right", "Contractual promise", "Moral obligation"],
 "A proprietary right is a right in the land itself, so it can bind successors in title. A personal right binds only the person who gave it."),
("A buyer who has exercised an option to purchase but not yet completed holds:",
 "An equitable interest", ["The legal title", "No interest in the land yet", "A registered lease"],
 "Once a binding contract is formed, the buyer has an equitable interest, which can be protected by lodging a caveat. Legal title passes only when the transfer is registered on completion."),
("Under State land grants, minerals beneath land in Singapore are generally:",
 "Reserved to the State", ["Owned by the owner", "Owned by the MCST", "Owned by the finder"],
 "State titles reserve minerals and similar rights to the State. The registered proprietor owns the land and its buildings, but not the minerals beneath it."),
("The two legal tests for whether an item has become a fixture are:",
 "Degree and purpose of annexation", ["Cost and age of the item", "Weight and size of the item", "Who paid for it and who installed it"],
 "Courts look at how firmly the item is attached and why it was attached. If it was fixed to improve the land permanently, it is likely a fixture that passes with the land."),
("A wardrobe built into the wall to improve a unit is, unless the contract says otherwise, most likely:",
 "A fixture that goes with the sale", ["A chattel the seller keeps", "A trade fixture the seller removes", "Excluded from every sale"],
 "A built-in item fixed to improve the property is a fixture and passes to the buyer. Parties should list fixtures and fittings in the option to avoid disputes."),
("A tenant's trade fixtures, such as shop counters fixed for the business, may usually be:",
 "Removed before the lease ends", ["Kept by the landlord free", "Removed any time after the lease expires", "Sold along with the building"],
 "Trade fixtures put in for the tenant's business may be removed by the tenant, usually before the lease ends, making good any damage. Left behind after the term, they may become the landlord's."),
("An estate granted by the State in perpetuity is known as:",
 "Freehold", ["99-year leasehold", "A temporary licence", "A life estate"],
 "A grant in perpetuity creates a freehold estate. State leases, such as 99-year or 999-year leases, are leasehold and the land returns to the State when they end."),
("Owners of HDB flats hold:",
 "A 99-year lease from HDB", ["A freehold estate", "A licence from the State", "A 999-year lease"],
 "HDB sells flats on 99-year leases, so flat owners are lessees of HDB. The lease runs down over time, which affects CPF usage and loans."),
("A Land Betterment Charge (LBC) is payable to the State when:",
 "Planning permission raises land value", ["Property tax falls into arrears", "An HDB flat changes owner", "A tenant renews the lease at market rent"],
 "Under the Land Betterment Charge Act 2021, LBC is payable when planning permission for a higher-value use or intensity, or lifting a title restriction, increases the land's value."),
("A temporary occupation licence (TOL) from the State gives the holder:",
 "Permission, not an estate", ["A registrable lease", "A freehold title", "A right to sell the land"],
 "A TOL is a licence to occupy state land for a period and purpose. It creates no estate in land and can be ended by the State."),
("Under the Land Acquisition Act 1966, the State may:",
 "Compulsorily acquire land", ["Cancel any caveat at will", "Set private rents", "Approve foreign buyers"],
 "The Act lets the State acquire private land compulsorily for public purposes, paying compensation. Foreign buyer approvals come under the Residential Property Act."),
("A road reserve line affecting a property usually means:",
 "Part may be needed for a road", ["The road is privately owned", "Parking outside is banned", "The owner must build the road himself"],
 "A road reserve marks land set aside for a future road or road widening. It can limit what an owner may build and may lead to acquisition, so buyers should check it."),
("A neighbour's wall built partly onto an owner's lot is an example of:",
 "Encroachment", ["An easement", "A restrictive covenant", "A caveat"],
 "Encroachment is when a structure intrudes onto another's land. It can affect ownership, use and value, and should be resolved before a sale."),
("What mainly distinguishes a lease from a licence?",
 "Exclusive possession", ["Payment of rent", "A written document", "A term over one year"],
 "A lease gives the tenant exclusive possession for a term and creates an interest in land. A licence is only permission to be there, even if a fee is paid."),
("A right of way over a neighbour's land to reach a road is an example of:",
 "An easement", ["A licence", "A covenant", "A mortgage"],
 "An easement is a right over another's land that benefits your land, such as a right of way or drainage. It attaches to the land, not to the owner personally."),
("Once SLA accepts a caveat, it stays in force, unless removed or lapsing earlier, for:",
 "Five years from lodgment", ["One year from lodgment", "Two years from lodgment", "Ten years from lodgment"],
 "SLA states that an accepted caveat is valid for five years from lodgment, and it can be extended. It may lapse earlier, for example after a notice to the caveator."),
("A restrictive covenant on a land title might:",
 "Bar building above two storeys", ["Let a neighbour park daily", "Secure a housing loan", "Give the State all the minerals below"],
 "A restrictive covenant limits how land may be used or built on, for the benefit of nearby land. A right to park is closer to an easement, and a loan is secured by a mortgage."),
("Which is one way a restrictive covenant can be discharged?",
 "Release by all who benefit", ["A new property tax notice", "Lapse after five years", "A sale of the burdened land to a new owner"],
 "A covenant ends if everyone entitled to its benefit releases it, or by court order or under the Land Titles Act procedures. A sale does not end it, since covenants run with the land."),
("A subsidiary strata certificate of title is issued for:",
 "A strata unit", ["A landed house lot", "An HDB rental flat", "A state land TOL"],
 "Each strata unit, such as a condominium apartment, has its own subsidiary strata certificate of title. A landed lot is shown on an ordinary certificate of title."),
("Which lease is registrable and noted on the land title?",
 "A lease over seven years", ["Any monthly tenancy", "A two-year tenancy", "A rental of one room"],
 "Under the Land Titles Act, leases exceeding seven years are registrable and appear on the title. Shorter tenancies are not registered."),
("Under the Land Titles Act, a registered proprietor's title is generally:",
 "Indefeasible, with exceptions", ["Valid only after a deed search", "Open to any older deed", "Unprotected until a caveat"],
 "Singapore's Torrens system makes the register conclusive: a registered title is indefeasible except in cases such as fraud. Buyers rely on the register instead of tracing old deeds."),
("Registered interests in land under the Land Titles Act generally rank by:",
 "Order of registration", ["Size of the loan", "Date of the contract", "Date the loan was approved"],
 "Priority among registered dealings follows the order in which they were lodged for registration. That is why lenders and buyers register or caveat promptly."),
("A buyer lodges a caveat soon after exercising an option mainly to:",
 "Warn others of his interest", ["Transfer the legal title", "Pay the stamp duty", "Cancel the seller's mortgage loan"],
 "A caveat protects an unregistered interest by stopping dealings that would defeat it and putting others on notice. The legal title passes only on registration of the transfer."),
]
